{
  "requirement": "For node reads, the SDK constructs a `Snapshot` from\nthe contents returned by `Domain::node_content` and their\n`NodeContents::fingerprint` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`, without another read.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Snapshot<T> {\n    pub content: T,\n    pub fingerprint: Fingerprint,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Snapshot<T> {\n    pub fn new(content: T, fingerprint: Fingerprint) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<T: Clone> Clone for Snapshot<T>;\nimpl<T: Debug> Debug for Snapshot<T>;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from `requirements.sdk.graph.snapshot`**\n1. For node reads, the SDK builds a `Snapshot` from the contents returned by `Domain::node_content`. It takes the fingerprint from `NodeContents::fingerprint`, and it does not read the node again.\n2. `Snapshot<T>` has public `content` and `fingerprint` fields and is `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n3. `Snapshot::new(content, fingerprint)` pairs the two values.\n4. `Snapshot<T>` implements `Clone` and `Debug`.\n\n**Test coverage**\n- **Node-read construction and fingerprint.**\n  - `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions]` asserts that `read_snapshot(&1).fingerprint == fp(\"before\")`. It also checks that the recorded `Dependency::Node` fingerprints are the ones in effect at each read.\n  - `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots]` runs over an empty string and a large multi-byte Unicode string. It asserts `snapshot.content.format()` and `snapshot.fingerprint == fp(original)`. It also asserts that the snapshot still holds the original content after the graph changes.\n  - `tests/contracts.rs#fn[identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot]` asserts that `snapshot.content.fingerprint() == snapshot.fingerprint`. It also asserts that the content and fingerprint stay \"before\" after the graph changes.\n- **No additional read.**\n  - In `direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots`, `read_snapshot` followed by `read` gives reads `[1, 1]`, so `read_snapshot` made exactly one read.\n  - `identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot` asserts reads `[1]` after `read_snapshot`, so building the snapshot, taking its fingerprint and reading `content` made no further read.\n  - `snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions` asserts exact read sequences across several reads.\n- **Agent paths.**\n  - `agent/tests.rs#fn[graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery]` checks that the agent's `read_node` records a `Dependency::Node` whose fingerprint comes from the read content.\n  - `explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots` checks the recorded `Dependency::Node` fingerprints for contents added explicitly to a request.\n  - `agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets` is also marked `{test}` for this requirement. It exercises edge snapshots, not node-content snapshots.\n- **Alternate domain.** `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` checks that fingerprints come from the retained contents and do not change when the underlying files change.\n- **Constructor and public fields.** `tests/contracts.rs#fn[callers_can_pair_content_with_its_fingerprint]` calls `Snapshot::new` from outside the crate. It asserts both public fields.\n\n**Remaining items**\n- No test calls `clone()` or `{:?}` on a `Snapshot`, and none checks `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- I read `graph.rs#struct[Snapshot]`. It is declared with `#[derive(Clone, Debug)]` and `#[non_exhaustive]`, which are declarative attributes. The derives have no conditional and no transformation of their own.\n- This falls under the testing criteria's exemption for code with nothing to branch on, so I do not count it as a gap.\n\nTogether, the tests check the construction, fingerprint pairing and single-read behaviour the requirement states, and they enforce each with assertions.",
  "references": "6783b2a4d0b4e2c98352e3255d1bb07342bc13f8749e2cee76ad5948eb461b69",
